artner a guy by the name  of tom schwartz for 20 years  who is my almost my you know we weren't  fit legally business partners  but my ceo would come up through the  ranks the guy would graduate business  school year after me  who was essential i i the business  wouldn't have been the same business  without it  and i think if you pull the curtain  behind a lot of institutions a lot of  families and a lot of a lot of  businesses you'll generally find that  symbiotic relationship exists  and elsewhere so i learned a lot from my  teammates  they really were like exceptional group  of people we were together for a long  time  and i owe them a great deal yeah that's  amazing  um one of the things that you guys did  very very well was you kind of continued  to  reinvent uh what was on the menu and  kind of innovate  and keep pushing forward in terms of the  offerings that you had  uh there's things like the munchkins or  the culadas and all kind of all this  stuff  talk a little bit about just what was  the process internally how intentional  is it versus kind of serendipitous  uh in terms of creating these new  products and really  scaling them to a national organization  so that you can  remain fresh and kind of at the top of  mind for consumers  early on it was serendipitous the  introduction of jumbo java which moving  from a six ounce to go cup of coffee to  a 10 ounce cup of coffee  came from a competitor uh or a marketing  head i hired from a competitor  munchkins was an idea that surfaced from  our hartford franchise on this  a lady by the name of edna demery the  wife of our franchise owner bob demary  she created munchkins but later on it  became very sophisticated and very  process oriented  basically we had a first-rate marketing  department  we had product managers for bakery  beverage  and donut categories their job was to  continually  search the marketplace the competition  the consumer  we took ideas every which way we ballot  tested them  we tested them 